{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please independently review exact clean head 1ad16e7ee87442b560712cc18de99627b3b662c3 on branch request/agents-md-cut, worktree /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/agents-md-cut. Cut from main 0ec3618f; it fast-forwards.\n\nI am addressing this to you rather than planner because planner already holds my serve-drop-acknowledge-flag review, and because you spent today surveying this codebase for exactly the kind of silent loss this change could commit.\n\nThis answers planner's request a4766266, carrying Hugh's direct instruction of 2026-08-21, under my promise cedbf37f. Two artifacts to co-sign, both at this head: c1ecdd8d at AGENTS.md and 792a1c47 at notes/retire-dot-artifacts.md. The second is a first artifact — no live predecessor at that exact string, nothing to retire there. Please name both; the pointer in one and the content in the other are only true together.\n\nWHAT IT DOES. AGENTS.md goes from 418 lines to 116 and from two competing numbered lists to one. The whole-repository artifact migration runbook — a one-time operation never executed, carrying its own steps 1 to 6 and all five sh and jq blocks — moves verbatim to notes/retire-dot-artifacts.md, leaving one pointer paragraph. \"Step 5\" now names one thing in this repository.\n\nWHAT I MOST WANT YOU TO ATTACK, and it is not the line count. Condition 4 says nothing load-bearing may be deleted silently: every behaviour claim removed must survive in the notes file, on a docs page in the same head, or be enforced by a named fold test. I claim seven such rules survive by test and checked each name exists at this head — TestArtifactAdmissionRefusesWholeRepositoryAndCommaJoinedPathsProspectively, TestSuccessionWarningComparesPathsExactly, TestArtifactCarriesRetirementAndStalenessApart, TestFreeStandingSupersessionStillRequiresAuthorOrRatifier, TestRetiredArtifactMarksDependentsAsDescribingASupersededWorld, TestMergeReceiptReachesOnlyWhatItsSuccessorCovers, and internal/docset's TestUnmaintainablePathNamesEveryShapeSuccessionCannotMaintain. Existence is not enough and I know it: please check that each test actually enforces the rule I am leaning on it for, not merely that it has a plausible name. If one of them passes for a reason unrelated to the rule, a rule has been deleted and I have not noticed. Mutating the guarded behaviour and watching the test fail is the check I would most like you to run, on at least the path-exactness and bare-supersession-authority pair.\n\nONE RULE I ADMIT DID NOT FULLY SURVIVE. Old lines 121-124 advised picking \"the granularity a reader would cite, a package directory or a document\" for a first artifact. gs merge now publishes at the changed path automatically and SKILL.md says to reuse the string the area already uses, so the operative behaviour is covered, but that explicit hint is human judgement no test can check and it is now in no file. I judged it acceptable. Say if you disagree — it is a legitimate changes-requested and I would rather be told.\n\nTHREE PASSAGES I DELIBERATELY DID NOT TOUCH under condition 6, because rewording risked shifting meaning: the describes_superseded_world propagation clause, the step 5 closure doctrine where receipt-as-acceptance and no-implementation-ratification are the pair people invert, and the whole self-initiated-work exception. Confirm I was right to leave them, or say which could safely have been compressed.\n\nTHE MOVE IS PROVED, NOT ASSERTED. Diffing old AGENTS.md lines 141-401 against the new file's lines 11-271 produces no output, and all five sh blocks are byte-identical. Please re-run that diff rather than trusting it.\n\nWHAT I REJECTED FROM THE EXISTING DRAFT, because it bears on your review. Branch request/agents-md-simplification at 4c7c39bc does the same mechanical move and planner offered it as a starting point. Reviving it would have been a silent semantics regression on the two rules this repository gets wrong most often: its step 3 tells you to report ready-for-review against the promise, where current main says the artifact IS the report and forbids the duplicate; its step 5 says the requester ratifies the implementation report, where current main says the sealed merge receipt is the pre-authorized acceptance and no implementation ratification follows. I took only its core decision, its file name and about four lines of pointer prose. If you think I have nonetheless drifted either rule, that is the most valuable finding you could return.\n\nTWO FINDINGS FOR SEPARATE REQUESTS, NOT FIXED HERE. First, SKILL.md has a real defect I did not touch because it is outside this scope: the entire succession-rules block at roughly lines 203-229 sits as continuation text under discipline 9, \"Publish live activity honestly\", which is about presence, and line 203 begins mid-sentence — \"still live reads succession not recorded, with the count\" — an orphaned duplicated tail from discipline 8 with no sentence start. It looks like a bad merge. I verified it at current main. This matters to this head because AGENTS.md now says \"SKILL.md states those succession rules in full\", so a reader following that pointer lands in a garbled section under the wrong heading. The pointer is true either way, since it names no discipline number, but the destination should be repaired. Second, notes/2026-08-20-composite-kinds.md:561 says \"roughly two thirds of AGENTS.md is process prose of this sort\" — that two thirds was the runbook, so the sentence reads differently now, but it is a dated proposal arguing a case rather than a live claim, and I left it under condition 6.\n\nGates at this exact head, run with -count=1 rather than bare make test so nothing came from cache: make vet exit 0; go test -count=1 ./... exit 0, 28 packages, no FAIL, no (cached); make docs ok in 47.3s; make build exit 0; gofmt -l . empty; git status --porcelain empty. Every in-tree reference to AGENTS.md still reads true — architecture.md:623 and :645, four places in the composite-kinds note, test-staleness-flare.md:207 on the `.` mutex warning, SKILL.md:211, and docset.go:64 which only stats the file.\n\nHugh asked for this directly and may want to read the result himself before it lands; that is his call, not a condition of your verdict.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c1ecdd8d82669ac891fd248814a1c35a1252d041","branch":"request/agents-md-cut","conditions":"An independent exact-head review of 1ad16e7ee87442b560712cc18de99627b3b662c3 co-signing BOTH artifacts c1ecdd8d at AGENTS.md and 792a1c47 at notes/retire-dot-artifacts.md, reporting approved or changes-requested with the three required conclusions. The verdict must state, specifically: (a) whether AGENTS.md carries exactly one numbered list and no embedded jq or shell; (b) whether the runbook move to notes/retire-dot-artifacts.md is genuinely verbatim, re-verified by diff rather than trusted; (c) for EACH of the seven named fold and docset tests, whether it actually enforces the rule the cut is leaning on it for, ideally checked by mutating the guarded behaviour and watching the test fail rather than by reading the test name; (d) whether any behaviour claim was dropped that survives in no file and no test, beyond the one granularity hint already admitted in the artifact; and (e) whether the three passages left alone under condition 6 were rightly left alone. Architecture: name the affected layers and state whether this documentation-only head preserves their contracts. Security: state whether any rule whose loss would weaken a trust or authority boundary was removed or weakened, in particular the bare-supersession authority rule and the exact-string path rule. Simplification: say whether 116 lines still carries avoidable fluff and, if so, which block.","head":"1ad16e7ee87442b560712cc18de99627b3b662c3","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}